Subject: six elisp packages that cannot be packaged as they stand
To: Thomas Klausner <wiz@NetBSD.org>

(DRAFT -- not sent.)

Hi Thomas,

This is separate from the framework thread because it is true of
pkgsrc as it is today and does not depend on any of that.  Please say
if you would rather have it as a PR; I am happy to send it that way
instead.

Going through the packages that spell ${PKGBASE} in their own Makefile
turned up five where the install writes ${PKGBASE} while the PLIST, or
the install itself, spells the bare name.  The two agree only while
EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX is empty, and it is not empty for xemacs.  I
built an xemacs-nox11 21.5.36 here and ran the five against it, with
your modules.mk and the packages untouched, so what follows is
measured rather than read.

Three of them fail, each in a different place:

  devel/ecb            stops before it builds anything.  WRKSRC comes
                       out as work/xemacs-ecb-<tag>, because
                       mk/fetch/github.mk has GITHUB_PROJECT?=
                       ${PKGBASE}, and the archive unpacks as
                       ecb-<tag>:
                         cd: Can't cd to .../work/xemacs-ecb-1330a44...

  editors/gnuserv      INSTALLATION_DIRS makes share/doc/${PKGBASE},
                       which is share/doc/xemacs-gnuserv, while
                       doc-install writes to share/doc/gnuserv, which
                       does not exist -- so that name becomes a
                       484-byte file holding whichever README was
                       installed last, the empty directory sits beside
                       it, and the file check stops the build.

  textproc/dictem      installs share/doc/xemacs-dictem/{COPYING,NEWS,
                       README} while the PLIST asks for
                       share/doc/dictem/*.

The other two, editors/matlab-mode and textproc/xslide, have the same
shape in their Makefiles -- matlab-mode creates
${PKGBASE:C|^xemacs-||} and installs into ${PKGBASE}, xslide uses
${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE} against a PLIST that says xslide -- but
I cannot tell you they fail there, because under xemacs they stop
earlier, byte-compiling:

  matlab-mode  Cannot open load file: "tempo"
  xslide       Cannot open load file: "sendmail"

with xemacs-packages installed.  So those two do not build under
xemacs today either, for a reason I have not chased.

One more of the same kind, found the same way.  devel/doxymacs names
no EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED, so it accepts emacs20, and under emacs20
it stops at

  doxymacs.el:243:11: Error: Symbol's function definition is void:
                      eieio-make-class-predicate

I thought that was a missing dependency on lang/eieio and it is not: I
deinstalled eieio and rebuilt under emacs30nox and it builds fine, so
Emacs 30 is using its own.  Emacs 20 simply has no eieio that provides
that function.  Whether the answer is to drop the old versions from
what doxymacs accepts, or something else, is yours rather than mine,
so I have not touched it.

devel/ecb is the one worth looking at first, because it takes
xemacs215 xemacs215nox xemacs214 xemacs214nox and nothing else, so
there is no version of it that works.

cad/dinotrace-mode spells ${PKGBASE} too and is fine.  It is the only
one of the nine whose PLIST also uses it, so both sides move together,
which is the whole distinction.

While installing xemacs I ran into something else.  editors/gnuserv
has

  CONFLICTS+=	xemacs-[0-9]* xemacs-current-[0-9]* # bin/gnuattach; bin/gnuclient

and neither pattern does what it looks like.  The packages are named
xemacs-21.4.25, xemacs-nox11-21.4.25, xemacs-21.5.36 and
xemacs-nox11-21.5.36, so xemacs-current-[0-9]* matches nothing at all,
and xemacs-[0-9]* does not cover the nox ones:

  pkg_info -e 'xemacs-[0-9]*'          rc=1
  pkg_info -e 'xemacs-current-[0-9]*'  rc=1
  pkg_info -e 'xemacs-nox11-[0-9]*'    rc=0  xemacs-nox11-21.5.36nb1

I found it by having it happen: pkg_add refused xemacs-nox11 over
bin/gnuattach with gnuserv already installed, which is the collision
the line is there to declare.  The diff below makes it
xemacs-nox11-[0-9]*.

A sixth is broken for a different reason, and this one I did build.
mail/mu cannot be packaged against an Emacs without D-Bus -- every nox
one, and an emacs30 with the dbus option turned off:

  pkg_create: can't stat .../site-lisp/mu4e/mu4e-dbus.elc

mu4e/meson.build asks the Emacs whether dbusbind is compiled in and
only byte-compiles mu4e-dbus.el when it is, while PLIST.emacs lists
the .elc with no condition.  The guard turns out not to be needed:
both requires in mu4e-dbus.el pass 'noerror, and with
emacs30-nox11-30.2nb1, which has no dbusbind, the file byte-compiles
with no output at all and the .elc loads.  With the guard removed,
mail/mu builds and the package has both mu4e-dbus.el and
mu4e-dbus.elc.

One more, which is recent rather than ancient.  misc/elscreen cannot
be built at all:

  misc/elscreen/Makefile 1.28   EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED= emacs20
                                .include ".../devel/apel/buildlink3.mk"
  devel/apel/Makefile    1.46   EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED+= emacs29 ...

Its only accepted Emacs is one its only elisp dependency refuses, and
the include is at top level rather than inside a condition.  The
dependency is real: elscreen.el requires alist and static, both apel.

I first read this as fallout from 2026-09-12 because both files carry
that date, but it is older.  In a 2 September copy apel is already at
1.45, from 26 August, with the same emacs29-and-up list, while
elscreen still accepted emacs21 emacs21nox emacs20.  So the overlap
went on 26 August, and the September commit only took emacs21 out of a
list that had already stopped working.  Its own comment says "Emacs 24
or later is not supported.  Try wip/elscreen-current instead", and
apel names emacs20 and emacs21 in EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE, so
there is no version where the two can meet.  I have not written a
patch for that one: whether elscreen should follow wip/elscreen-current,
or be marked, is not mine to decide.

Three others reach apel the same way and are only partly affected,
because apel's buildlink3.mk opens with .if ${EMACS_FLAVOR} ==
"emacs", so under xemacs the dependency is simply not there:

  misc/lookup              emacs20 fails; xemacs214/215 are fine
  www/emacs-w3m            emacs20 fails; emacs29 and up are fine
  www/emacs-w3m-snapshot   the same

That flavour guard is worth knowing about before counting this sort of
thing: editors/mule-ucs guards its buildlink with .if ${EMACS_FLAVOR}
== "emacs" && ${EMACS_VERSION_MAJOR} < 22, so a plain read of who
depends on whom reports pairs that do not exist at the version being
built.

While I was in these Makefiles I went looking for declarations that no
longer describe anything, since that is the kind of thing that rots
quietly.  Two sorts turned up.

devel/semi is the serious one.  Its options.mk still has

  .if !empty(PKG_OPTIONS:Msemi-bbdb)
  .include "../../misc/bbdb2/buildlink3.mk"

and misc/bbdb2 went out with emacs21, so with that option set the
package does not fail to build -- it fails to parse:

  bmake: ".../devel/semi/options.mk" line 14:
         Could not find ../../misc/bbdb2/buildlink3.mk
  bmake: Fatal errors encountered -- cannot continue

misc/bbdb3 is still there, so the obvious repair is to point the
option at it, and that is what I tried first.  It builds -- and it
would not work.  mime-bbdb.el is BBDB 2 code, and against bbdb3 it
byte-compiles with

  bbdb-record-getprop, bbdb-record-putprop, bbdb-search-simple,
  bbdb-pop-up-bbdb-buffer, bbdb-record-edit-notes, bbdb-annotate-notes

all "not known to be defined".  I went through bbdb-3.2.2a's lisp and
none of the six is there under any of those names, so the package
would install a file that fails the moment it is used.

So the diff below takes the option out instead, with the reasoning in
a comment where the block was.  devel/semi then builds, and setting
PKG_OPTIONS.semi=semi-bbdb no longer does anything rather than
stopping make.  If someone wants the feature back, what is missing is
a port of mime-bbdb.el to BBDB 3, not a line in options.mk.  I left
PLIST_VARS and the PLIST entry alone; with the option gone the line is
simply never emitted.

The other sort is CONFLICTS lines that match no package.  I checked
each with pkg_admin pmatch rather than by eye, which was worth doing
because one I was sure about turned out to be fine (mail/elm's PKGNAME
is elm-2.5.8nb5, not the elm2.5.8 its DISTNAME suggests).

  devel/pcl-cvs      CONFLICTS= emacs-21.*
                     emacs21's PKGNAME was emacs21-21.4anb49, which
                     that never matched; and it is gone now anyway
  inputmethod/skk    CONFLICTS= ddskk-[0-9]*        no such package
  mail/mew           CONFLICTS+= mew-xemacs-[0-9]*  no such package
  inputmethod/       CONFLICTS= tamago-anthy-[0-9]*
    tamago-tsunagi   only in wip

None of these breaks anything today -- a CONFLICTS that matches
nothing simply does nothing.  They are worth a look because the thing
they were guarding may still be true.  editors/gnuserv above is the
case where it was.

The diffs are below.  I checked them in check mode against a fresh
trunk checkout this morning: they apply with no offset and no fuzz.
For mail/mu the file patches/patch-mu4e_meson.build already exists, so
that one is a change to an existing patch rather than a new one, and
its distinfo line moves with it.

Showta
